#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# find_integer_null_vector

#% text
# `find_integer_null_vector([x1, ..., xn])` gives integers c1, ..., cn, not all zero, with c1 x1 + ... + cn xn = 0 to the precision of the numbers.

#% text
# `find_integer_null_vector(xs, d)` only accepts a relation of norm at most d.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The search is the PSLQ algorithm at the precision of the input (machine numbers 53 bits, numbers of `n(x, d)` their own); every relation is checked again before it is returned.

#% item
# When the relation is as large as relations that hold for any numbers at that precision, a message says it may be an artifact; when none exists below the bound PSLQ proves, the result is a failure with a message.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A relation among 1, sqrt(2) and 2:

#% cell
find_integer_null_vector([1, sqrt(2.0), 2.0])
#% output
#= [2, 0, -1]

#% text
# Machin's formula for pi, at 30 digits:

#% cell
find_integer_null_vector(n([pi/4, atan(1/5), atan(1/239)], 30))
#% output
#= [1, -4, 1]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Logarithms:

#% cell
find_integer_null_vector(n([1, log(2), log(3), log(6)], 40))
#% output
#= [0, 1, 1, -1]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# No relation of small size:

#% cell
find_integer_null_vector(n([zeta(3), pi^3, 1], 50))
#% output
#| find_integer_null_vector: no integer relation found within the precision of the input
#! failure(not_found, find_integer_null_vector)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `root_approximant`, `rationalize`, `minimal_polynomial`, `n`
